# CURATED signatures — hand-written, not generated.
#
# Every signature here describes a type tony-codegen cannot describe correctly today. They
# are kept separate from the generated files, and reported separately by the endpoint, so a
# reader can tell which part of the published schema is derived from the code and which part
# is a human's claim about it. A curated signature can drift from the code; a generated one
# cannot. That difference is worth surfacing rather than hiding behind a uniform document.
#
# Remove an entry as soon as codegen can produce it, and the endpoint will serve the
# generated one instead. That has already happened once: verse-entity and verse-event were
# curated because the schema mapper did not consult TextMarshaler and called entity.Ref an
# int; go-tony v0.0.93 fixed it, so they are generated now and their entries are gone.

# trigger.Match — the charter's selector grammar. codec=custom, so codegen emits an empty
# `define`, which would publish the most-authored type in the system as having no shape at
# all. Described here as what it is: an object of match dimensions, each of which may be a
# literal or a tony match operator, which is why the values are open.
context:
- tony-format/context
- tony-base: github.com/signadot/tony-format/go-tony/schema
define:
  system: .[string]
  kind: .[string]
  id: .[tony-base:ir]
  op: .[tony-base:ir]
  payload: .[tony-base:ir]
  fields: .[object(tony-base:ir)]
  entity: .[object(tony-base:ir)]
signature:
  name: verse-match
